  "group_by_fix_2026_07_06": {
    "summary": "SPARQL11.Algebra.fst's group_by/add_to_groups accumulated each group's solutions via Lh.append_tr g.g_solutions [mu] -- O(current group size) per row, because append_tr walks its entire first argument. GROUP BY ?p over gene.ttl produces 6 groups, one holding 759,263 of 888,949 rows; appending onto that one group's growing list gave ~10^11 operations total, matching the >600s timeout on all three factoidal paths recorded above. Fix: cons the new solution onto the front of the matched group (O(1)) during the fold, then reverse each group's solution list exactly once after the fold completes (group_by), restoring original row order in O(n) total instead of O(n) per row. Verified under F* 2025.12.15 / z3 4.13.3, no --lax, no --admit_smt_queries. Subset growth curve (gene.ttl prefix subsets, same query, same unfixed vs fixed binary): unfixed 10k/50k/100k/200k lines -> 0.18s/2.61s/16.11s/112.48s (exponent trending toward ~2.6-2.8, consistent with O(n^2) plus GC pressure at scale); fixed -> 0.13s/0.70s/1.50s/3.21s (exponent ~1.1, linear). Full 888,949-triple corpus, fixed: in-memory fast-path 27.17s, in-memory forced-slow-path 26.74s, COTTAS on-disk 109.93s -- all three now finish and all three produce the answer_sha256 e709bd9ceadd3c45, byte-identical to Jena/pyoxigraph/rdflib's answer for this query. This is order-of-magnitude, not literal, parity with Jena in-memory's 2.05s (~13x slower) -- the quadratic cliff is gone but the linear constant factor of GROUP BY partitioning is its own, separate, smaller optimization opportunity not addressed by this fix.",
    "mechanism": "formal/fstar/SPARQL11.Algebra.fst add_to_groups (~line 3115-3140) and group_by (~line 3170-3190)",
